@meta
id: 26-14
title: The jobs vim's regex is bad at
chapter: 26
type: lesson
skills: filter-bang
par: 40
minutes: 7
vscode: type the numbers in by hand and renumber them when a step moves
open: runbook.md

@brief
Six steps in an incident runbook, in the right order and unnumbered. On
call, people say "I got to step four", so they need numbers: `1. ` at the
front of the first step, `2. ` at the front of the second, and so on.

Vim's substitute cannot count. `:s` can match every one of those lines in
one command and it can put the same text in front of all of them, but it
has no idea which match it is on — a counter needs `:g` plus a variable,
which is chapter 24 material written the long way.

`awk` counts for free. `NR` is the number of the line it is reading, and
that numbering starts at 1 for whatever you feed it, not at 1 for the
file. The steps are lines 6 to 11.

@keys
| `:{range}!awk '{print NR". "$0}'` | prefix each line with its number |
| `$0` | in awk, the whole line |
| `NR` | in awk, how many lines it has read so far |
| `:%!sed 's/old/new/'` | sed, for when it is easier than `:s` |

@hints
Range, bang, command — the same shape as the first lesson in this
chapter. The command is the part that is new.
--
`awk` takes its program as one quoted argument. Inside it,
`print NR". "$0` prints the counter, a literal dot and space, then the
line.
--
`:6,11!awk '{print NR". "$0}'` and Enter, then `:wq`.

@solution
    :6,11!awk '{print NR". "$0}'<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

The point is not awk. The point is knowing when to stop.

Everything in vim's own language — `:s`, `:g`, `:normal`, macros — works
on one line at a time with no memory of the lines before it. That covers
most editing. The moment a job needs *state* across lines, vim's tools
get clumsy and the shell's get simple, because counting, summing,
grouping and joining are what these programs were written for. Numbering
is the clearest case: awk keeps the counter for you, and it renumbers
correctly the day somebody inserts a step in the middle, which is when
hand-typed numbers rot.

A few more where the shell is plainly the shorter answer:

    :%!awk '{s+=$3} END {print s}'    sum a column, buffer replaced by the total
    :%!awk '!seen[$0]++'              drop duplicate lines, keep the order
    :'<,'>!sort -t, -k3 -rn           sort a CSV by the third field, numerically
    :%!sed 's/  */ /g'                collapse runs of spaces
    :%!tail -r                        reverse the file, on the systems that have it

And two pieces of grit. In the second one, `awk '!seen...'` starts with a
`!`, which vim expands to *the previous shell command* in any `:!` line —
escape it as `\!` or you will run something you did not mean to. The same
goes for `%` and `#`, which become the current and alternate file names.
Escape them when you mean them literally.

The other is that `awk` and `sed` are not vim's regex. `\v` means nothing
to them, `\(` groups in sed but `(` groups in `sed -E`, and `\zs` does not
exist anywhere but vim. When the pattern is the hard part, keep it in
vim's `:s`, where you have the syntax memorised. When the *logic* is the
hard part, hand the lines over.

@start runbook.md
# Incident runbook: duplicate charges

Work through these in order. The steps are numbered so that whoever is on
call can say which one they stopped at.

Freeze the payment consumer
Pull the duplicate charge ids from the ledger
Refund the second charge in each pair
Reconcile the ledger against the gateway
Post the incident note in the payments channel
Unfreeze the consumer

@expected runbook.md
# Incident runbook: duplicate charges

Work through these in order. The steps are numbered so that whoever is on
call can say which one they stopped at.

1. Freeze the payment consumer
2. Pull the duplicate charge ids from the ledger
3. Refund the second charge in each pair
4. Reconcile the ledger against the gateway
5. Post the incident note in the payments channel
6. Unfreeze the consumer
